# Line-ending and binary policy.
#
# This file exists because of a near-miss. `core.autocrlf` is true on the machine this repository
# is developed on, there was no policy here, and `git add data/samples/ap77c.pdf` warned that it
# would rewrite LF to CRLF. Git normally detects a binary by looking for NUL bytes and leaves it
# alone, but that fixture is a minimal uncompressed PDF written by `scripts/make_hard_sample.py`
# and contains none — so it read as text. Rewriting its line endings shifts every byte offset in
# the trailing `xref` table, which is how a PDF locates its own objects. The file would still open
# in some viewers and would fail in pdfplumber, on the CI runner only, with a parse error that
# names nothing.
#
# So: declare the policy rather than rely on content sniffing.

# Everything not named below is text, normalised to LF in the repository. Working copies keep
# whatever the platform prefers; the committed bytes are identical either way, which is what makes a
# Windows checkout and a Linux runner agree on a file hash.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries. `-text` disables all conversion in both directions.
*.pdf   -text
*.png   -text
*.jpg   -text
*.jpeg  -text
*.gif   -text
*.webp  -text
*.ico   -text
*.woff  -text
*.woff2 -text
*.xlsx  -text
*.zip   -text

# Windows-only scripts, which genuinely need CRLF to run.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts and anything the CI runner executes must keep LF, or bash fails with a
# `\r: command not found` that reads as a syntax error in the script rather than a checkout problem.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Lockfiles are generated. Marking them keeps a diff from being attributed to a reviewer.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
